\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62~CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air in cavity~(E) expands thermally, pressurising the water-filled reservoir~(H) and forcing water through pipe~(L) into cauldron~(M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes counterweight~(R), opening the doors; the process reverses when the fire is extinguished. Adapted from~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {herondoor}.}}{2}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:herondoor}{{1.1}{2}{Automatic mechanism for temple door actuation designed by Heron of Alexandria (62~CE). When the altar fire is lit, the air in cavity~(E) expands thermally, pressurising the water-filled reservoir~(H) and forcing water through pipe~(L) into cauldron~(M). The resulting weight displacement overcomes counterweight~(R), opening the doors; the process reverses when the fire is extinguished. Adapted from~\cite {herondoor}}{figure.caption.6}{}}
